{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lieut. Samuel Leavitt (1641–1707) was an early colonial settler of Exeter, New Hampshire, one of the four original towns in the colony of New Hampshire, where Leavitt later served as a delegate to the General Court as well as Lieutenant in the New Hampshire Militia, and subsequently as member of the New Hampshire House of Representatives.","title":"Samuel Leavitt"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The recipient of large grants of land in Rockingham County, Leavitt held positions of authority within the colonial province.","title":"Samuel Leavitt"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Dennis Pennington (May 18, 1776 – September 2, 1854) was a farmer and a stonemason who became known for his many years in public office as an early legislator in the Indiana Territory and in Indiana's General Assembly as a representative of Harrison County, Indiana.","title":"Dennis Pennington"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Pennington, a member of the Whig Party, became the first speaker of the Indiana territorial legislature's lower house in 1810, served as the territory's census enumerator in 1815, and represented Harrison County as one of its five delegates to the constitutional convention of 1816.","title":"Dennis Pennington"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Pennington was the first speaker of the Indiana Senate (1816 to 1818), and served in the state legislature for eighteen years, which included five years in the Indiana House of Representatives and thirteen years in the Indiana Senate.","title":"Dennis Pennington"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" His major political contributions relate to his strong opposition to slavery.","title":"Dennis Pennington"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Pennington ran unsuccessfully for Indiana's Lieutenant Governor in 1825.","title":"Dennis Pennington"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" In addition to his service in the state legislature, Penning was a Harrison County sheriff and a justice of the peace, a trustee of Indiana University, and a member of the Gr