{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"First and Last Things is a 1908 work of philosophy by H. G. Wells setting forth his beliefs in four "books" entitled "Metaphysics," "Of Belief," "Of General Conduct," and "Some Personal Things."","title":"First and Last Things"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Parts of the book were published in the "Independent Magazine" in July and August 1908.","title":"First and Last Things"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Wells revised the book extensively in 1917, in response to his religious conversion, but later published a further revision in 1929 that restored much of the book to its earlier form.","title":"First and Last Things"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Its main intellectual influences are Darwinism and certain German thinkers Wells had read, such as August Weismann.","title":"First and Last Things"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The pragmatism of William James, who had become a friend of Wells, was also an influence.","title":"First and Last Things"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster is a 1924 biography of Frederick William Sanderson (1857-1922) by H. G. Wells.","title":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is the only biography Wells wrote.","title":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Sanderson was a personal friend, having met Wells in 1914 when his sons George Philip ('Gip'), born in 1901, and Frank Richard, born in 1903, became pupils at Oundle School, of which Sanderson was headmaster from 1892 to 1922.","title":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" After Sanderson died while giving a lecture at University College London at which he was introduced by Wells, the famous author agreed to help produce a biography to raise money for the school.","title":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" But in December 1922, after disagreements emerged with Sanderson's widow about his approach to the subject, Wells withdrew from the official biography (published in 1923 as "Sanderson of Oundle"; Wells wrote much of the text but the volume was published without listing an author) and published his own work separately.","title":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pierre Martory (December 1, 1920 – October 5, 1998) was a French poet whose influence on New York School poets was quiet but profound.","title":"Pierre Martory"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" His work was admired by Frank O'Hara, James Schuyler, Harry Mathews, and others, and translated extensively by John Ashbery, with whom he shared his life in Paris for nearly a decade.","title":"Pierre Martory"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" His work has appeared in many books in both England and the United States, as well as in "The New Yorker" and "Poetry".","title":"Pierre Martory"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Martory’s personal “charm,” the poet Ann Lauterbach once said, “devolved back to the original meaning of ‘spell.","title":"Pierre Martory"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":"’” However, as Ashbery has noted, “Both the humor and the sadness in his poems are always rendered with an unemphatic clarity that is certainly Mozartian.”","title":"Pierre Martory"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Trevor Winkfield (born 1944) is a British-born artist and writer.","title":"Trevor Winkfield"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Drawing upon his interest in both modernist literary movements and medieval architecture and pageantry, Winkfield has collaborated with many contemporary poets and writers, including John Ashbery, Harry Mathews, James Schuyler, and Ron Padgett.","title":"Trevor Winkfield"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Jack Shoemaker (born 1946) is an American editor and publisher, and current editorial director and vice-president at Counterpoint Press in Berkeley, California.","title":"Jack Shoemaker"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Shoemaker has edited and published books under several imprints, including North Point, Pantheon Books, Shoemaker & Hoard, and Counterpoint.","title":"Jack Shoemaker"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Shoemaker has published books by Guy Davenport, Romulus Linney, Gary Snyder, Wendell Berry, Evan S. Connell, MFK Fisher, James Salter, Gina Berriault, Reynolds Price, W.S. Merwin, Michael Palmer, Donald Hall, Anne Lamott, Kay Boyle, Gary Nabhan, Jane Vandenburgh, Carole Maso, and Robert Aitken.","title":"Jack Shoemaker"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Shoemaker supports author-driven literary publishing ventures and mindfulness and political awareness in publishing.","title":"Jack Shoemaker"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Shoemaker was one of the first American publisher of Thich Nhat Hanh, and a major publisher of Wendell Berry.","title":"Jack Shoemaker"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Locus Solus was an American journal of experimental poetry and prose that published four issues in 1961 and 1962, one a double issue.","title":"Locus Solus (journal)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The magazine was edited by the poets John Ashbery, Kenneth Koch, and James Schuyler, all of whom contributed to its four issues.","title":"Locus Solus (journal)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The content was completely in English but the journal was published in France (in Lans-en-Vercors) by Harry Mathews.","title":"Locus Solus (journal)"}],"peer_messages":["title":"Pierre Martory"}
