{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"In Renaissance London, playing company was the usual term for a company of actors.","title":"Playing company"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" These companies were organized around a group of ten or so shareholders (or "sharers"), who performed in the plays but were also responsible for management.","title":"Playing company"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The sharers employed "hired men" that is, the minor actors and the workers behind the scenes.","title":"Playing company"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The major companies were based at specific theatres in London; the most successful of them, William Shakespeare's company the King's Men, had the open-air Globe Theatre for summer seasons and the enclosed Blackfriars Theatre in the winters.","title":"Playing company"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The Admiral's Men occupied the Rose Theatre in the 1590s, and the Fortune Theatre in the early 17th century.","title":"Playing company"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Admiral's Men (also called the Admiral's company, more strictly, the Earl of Nottingham's Men; after 1603, Prince Henry's Men; after 1612, the Elector Palatine's Men or the Palsgrave's Men) was a playing company or troupe of actors in the Elizabethan and Stuart eras.","title":"Admiral's Men"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is generally considered the second most important acting troupe of English Renaissance theatre (after the company of Shakespeare, the Lord Chamberlain's or King's Men).","title":"Admiral's Men"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 2011 Hotan attack was a bomb-and-knife attack that occurred in Hotan, Xinjiang, China on July 18, 2011.","title":"2011 Hotan attack"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" According to witnesses, the assailants were a group of 18 young Uyghur men who opposed the local government's campaign against the full-face Islamic veil, which had grown popular among older Hotan women in 2009 but were also used in a series of violent crimes.","title":"2011 Hotan attack"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The men occupied a police station on Nuerbage Street at noon, killing two security guards with knives and bombs and taking eight hostages.","title":"2011 Hotan attack"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The attackers then yelled religious slogans, including ones associated with Jihadism, as they replaced the Chinese flag on top of a police station with another flag, the identity of which is disputed.","title":"2011 Hotan attack"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Admiral Theatre in Chicago, Illinois opened in 1927 as a vaudeville house.","title":"Admiral Theatre"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" it was designed by Gallup and Joy and acquired by the Balaban and Katz circuit.","title":"Admiral Theatre"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Admiral closed sometime in the late 1950s, and remained shuttered for many years until opening in 1969 as an all-cartoon venue.","title":"Admiral Theatre"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Unable to draw the crowds necessary to remain open, the Admiral closed again.","title":"Admiral Theatre"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In the early 1970s, the Admiral was opened as an adult movie house.","title":"Admiral Theatre"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" After receiving a facelift in the 1980s, the Admiral continues to thrive as an adult venue and gentlemen’s club.","title":"Admiral Theatre"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" While the interior has been drastically altered, the facade is in remarkably good shape.","title":"Admiral Theatre"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Diederichs's stone (German: Diederichsstein, ) was a German monument in the city of Qingdao to commemorate Admiral Otto von Diederichs and the German occupation of the Kiautschou Bay concession on November 14, 1897.","title":"Diederichs's stone"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The monument was dedicated on November 21, 1898 by Prince Henry of Prussia.","title":"Diederichs's stone"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was located at an elevation of 98m, about halfway up the southwestern slope of the Signal Hill, the official German name of the mountain at the time was "Diederichsberg" ).","title":"Diederichs's stone"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Its most prominent feature was a plate decorated with the imperial eagle of the German Empire and the inscription "For him who won for Kaiser and Reich the land all around, let this rock be named Diederichs's stone" ("Der hier für Kaiser warb u. [und] Reich ringsher das Land, nach ihm sei dieser Felsen Diederichsstein genannt").","title":"Diederichs's stone"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Below the plate was a rock inscription that read "In this place on November 14th, 1897, Admiral v.[von] Diederichs took possession of the Kiautschou territory" ("Am 14.","title":"Diederichs's stone"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" November 1897 ergriff an dieser Stelle der Admiral v.[von] Diederichs Besitz vom Kiautschou Gebiet").","title":"Diederichs's stone"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" A separate Chinese inscription was located to the right-hand side of the German text.","title":"Diederichs's stone"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The entire monument stood about 5 meters tall.","title":"Diederichs's stone"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" After Japan occupied Qingdao in November 1914, a Japanese inscription (executed using Chinese characters: 大正三年十一月七日, "November 7 of the third year of the Taishō period") was placed across the imperial eagle.","title":"Diederichs's stone"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" When Japan handed Qingdao back to the China on December 10, 1922, the monument was dismantled and parts taken to a military museum in Tokyo.","title":"Diederichs's stone"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Spanish Golden Age theatre refers to theatre in Spain roughly between 1590 and 1681.","title":"Spanish Golden Age theatre"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Spain emerged as a European power after it was unified by the marriage of Ferdinand II of Aragon and Isabella I of Castile in 1469 and then claimed for Christianity at the Siege of Granada in 1492.","title":"Spanish Golden Age theatre"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The sixteenth and seventeenth centuries saw a monumental increase in the production of live theatre as well as the in importance of the arts within Spanish society.","title":"Spanish Golden Age theatre"}],"peer_messages":["Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"In Renaissance London, playing company was the usual term for a company of actors.","title":"Playing company"}
