{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Finally is the debut album by American singer CeCe Peniston, released on January 28, 1992 by A&M Records.","title":"Finally (CeCe Peniston album)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Prior to the release of this album, Peniston released her debut single "Finally", which topped the US "Billboard" Hot Dance Music Club Play chart on October 26, 1991, peaking eventually at number five on the "Billboard" Hot 100 and at number two in the UK Singles Chart.","title":"Finally (CeCe Peniston album)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The album yielded two additional singles which achieved dance number one status in the US; "We Got a Love Thang", co-written by Chantay Savage, and "Keep On Walkin'", written in collaboration with Kym Sims.","title":"Finally (CeCe Peniston album)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Both songs entered the UK Singles Chart top 10 and the "Billboard" Hot 100 top 20 in the US.","title":"Finally (CeCe Peniston album)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Despite the success of the singles, the album itself climbed only to number seventy on the US "Billboard" 200.","title":"Finally (CeCe Peniston album)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" However, during its thirty-six weeks long presence in the chart it sold over 554,000 copies in the US.","title":"Finally (CeCe Peniston album)"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The album peaked at number ten on the UK Albums Chart.","title":"Finally (CeCe Peniston album)"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The total worldwide sales of the album surpassed 3,000,000 units.","title":"Finally (CeCe Peniston album)"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Two further tracks were released from the album which were more in the R&B field; "Inside That I Cried" charted at number ten in the US R&B chart and at number forty-two in the UK, while "Crazy Love" peaked at number thirty-one in the US and at number forty-four in the UK.","title":"Finally (CeCe Peniston album)"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" The album was part of the resurgence of dance music in the United States during the mid-1990s.","title":"Finally (CeCe Peniston album)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Termites are a 5-piece psychobilly musical group from Scotland that formed in the 1980s.","title":"The Termites"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Their most recent CD, "Kicked In The Teeth" on Crazy Love Records, was released in 2008.","title":"The Termites"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Crazy Love Tour was the fourth concert tour by Canadian singer Michael Bublé.","title":"Crazy Love Tour"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The tour supported his sixth studio album, "Crazy Love".","title":"Crazy Love Tour"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Visiting the Americas, Europe, Australia, Asia, and Africa, the tour has played to over one million spectators in nearly 21 countries.","title":"Crazy Love Tour"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The tour has received remarkable praise from both music critics and spectators of the show.","title":"Crazy Love Tour"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In 2010, Pollstar announced the trek became the sixth highest grossing tour worldwide, earning over $100 million with 99 sold out shows.","title":"Crazy Love Tour"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Additionally was the fourth highest grossing tour in North America—bringing in over $60 million in revenue with 50 sold out shows.","title":"Crazy Love Tour"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The tour ranked 16th in Pollstar's "Top 50 Worldwide Tour (Mid-Year)", earning over 30 million dollars in 2011.","title":"Crazy Love Tour"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" At the conclusion of 2011, the tour placed eleventh on Billboard's annual "Top 25 Tours", earning nearly $50 million with 57 shows in 2011.","title":"Crazy Love Tour"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Crazy Love: Overwhelmed by a Relentless God is a 2008 New York Times bestselling Christian book written by Francis Chan and published by David C Cook.","title":"Crazy Love: Overwhelmed by a Relentless God"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is co-authored by Danae Yankoski with a foreword by Chris Tomlin.","title":"Crazy Love: Overwhelmed by a Relentless God"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The book inspired the titular song for the album Crazy Love by Hawk Nelson and in 2009, won the Retailers Choice Award for the best Christian Living book.","title":"Crazy Love: Overwhelmed by a Relentless God"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Haven't Met You Yet" is the first single from Canadian singer Michael Bublé's fourth album, "Crazy Love", released on August 31, 2009.","title":"Haven't Met You Yet"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" According to Bublé, the single and its official music video are "about everyone's dream of finding a relationship and love."","title":"Haven't Met You Yet"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Bublé co-wrote "Haven't Met You Yet" with Alan Chang and Amy Foster-Gilles, and dedicated it to his then fiancée and now wife, Luisana Lopilato (who appears as his love interest in the music video).","title":"Haven't Met You Yet"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""This Crazy Love" is a song written by Roger Murrah and James Dean Hicks, and recorded by American country music group The Oak Ridge Boys.","title":"This Crazy Love"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was released in June 1987 as the second single from the album "Where the Fast Lane Ends".","title":"This Crazy Love"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "This Crazy Love" was The Oak Ridge Boys' fifteenth number one on the country chart.","title":"This Crazy Love"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The single went to number one for one week and spent a total of fifteen weeks on the country chart.","title":"This Crazy Love"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" It was released following the departure William Lee Golden in March 1987.","title":"This Crazy Love"}],"peer_messages":["":0,"text":"\"This Crazy Love\" is a song written by Roger Murrah and James Dean Hicks, and recorded by American country music group The Oak Ridge Boys.","title":"This Crazy Love"}
