{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pre-Creedence is a compilation album by The Golliwogs which changed its name to Creedence Clearwater Revival (CCR) in 1968.","title":"Pre-Creedence"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" This album was released in 1975 after the band had disbanded.","title":"Pre-Creedence"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Dean Ellis Kohler is an American rock musician from Portsmouth, Virgina who has been active in various groups and as a solo artist since 1962.","title":"Dean Kohler"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" That year he formed Dean & the Mustangs, who later changed their name and recorded demos as the Satellites.","title":"Dean Kohler"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 1966 Kohler was drafted into the army as an MP in the Vietnam War.","title":"Dean Kohler"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" While stationed in Qui Nhơn, he formed the group, the Electrical Banana, who recorded several songs in an army tent, amongst which a version of Lou Reed's "There She Goes Again" and Kohler's original, "She's Gone" were pressed as an acetate and have come to the attention of garage rock enthusiasts.","title":"Dean Kohler"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In 1968, when he returned from duty to Portsmouth, he assembled a backing band and recorded the single "Goosebury Pie".","title":"Dean Kohler"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Later in 1968, he formed the band, the Soft Light, and by the end of the decade the hard rock group, Mad Max (not to be confused with the German group of the same name), who remained active into the 1970s.","title":"Dean Kohler"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" He chronicled his Vietnam War experiences in "Rock 'N' Roll Soldier: A Memoir", published in 2009.","title":"Dean Kohler"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Kohler's recordings with the Satellites and the Electrical Banana are included on the compilation, "Aliens, Psychos & Wild Things, Volume 1", and "Goosebury Pie" on "Essential Pebbles, Volume 2."","title":"Dean Kohler"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Without Grace are a rock band from Guildford, Surrey.","title":"Without Grace"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" They have recently announced on their web page that they will be taking a break from the band to pursue any other careers.","title":"Without Grace"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "Without Grace", originally called Jelutong, met at the age of 13 at school.","title":"Without Grace"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Jon and Matt where the first members of the band to play together, playing in bands from the age of 11.","title":"Without Grace"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" After leaving school the band changed their name to "Without Grace" and signed to Management company "Hug Management".","title":"Without Grace"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" In 2004 the band signed to Universal and recorded their debut single 20 Yards Away which was never released after the band was dropped when their A&R left.","title":"Without Grace"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The band, still determined, recorded their debut album at Invincible Studios and released it in 2006 through Genius Twin.","title":"Without Grace"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The band split in 2007 due to the members drifting apart musically.","title":"Without Grace"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Singer Markus formed a solo project, entitled Spotlight Cannibal, keeping Jonty on drums, but more recently he has been fronting the hard rock band The Royal Cartel who have supported Marillion.","title":"Without Grace"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" Matt went on to from Flame to Fire with previous members of rock band Radiate, Steve Bradfield and G Rubery.","title":"Without Grace"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Hangmen were an American garage rock band from Rockville, Maryland who were active in the 1960s.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In an effort to distinguish themselves from other American groups and tie-in with the popular British Invasion, they lured Scottish vocalist Dave Ottley to join the group.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Eventually they became the most popular band in the Washington, DC area, having a huge regional hit with "What a Girl Can’t Do", that appeared on Monument Records, but had actually been recorded by an earlier local group, the Reekers, whose membership included two future Hangmen, Tom Guernsey and Bob Berberich.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The single was nonetheless credited to the Hangmen.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The song became so popular that the band was greeted with near "Beatlemanic" adoration from fans, whose enthusiasm on one occasion erupted into a near riot.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The group followed-up with the single "Faces", which featured more aggressive sound, replete with a snide vocal from Ottley and a razor-like fuzz-driven guitar riff.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Ottley shortly thereafter departed from the band and was replaced by Tony Taylor.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The group, now with Taylor, went to Nashville to record the album "Bittersweet", which displayed a more eclectic and relaxed approach, which, despite its closing song, a raucous version of Van Morrison's' Gloria", stood in marked contrast to their previous efforts.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" The album's opening cut was sitar-embellished version of "Dream Baby, which was also released as a single.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" After additional rounds of lineup changes and in an effort to emphasize their increasingly psychedelic orientation, the Hangmen changed their name to the Button.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" As the Button, they taped an unreleased set of songs for RCA Records in New York, but subsequently changed their name to Graffiti and recorded briefly for ABC Records.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Still Life was a 3-piece rock band originally from Moorpark, California and then the San Fernando Valley, California.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" They are credited for helping pioneer the first wave of what is often referred to as emo/emocore /screamo bands in this subgenre that is rooted in punk rock.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" They started in the late 80s with the name Monster Club.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" They changed their name in 1991 after the departure of the original lead singer Rick Rodney who joined the band Strife.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" They were active until 2003 when all of their instruments were stolen from Chris Pitzel's truck parked on the street in front of their house after a show.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Former Members Paul Rauch and David Pitzel have started a new band named Old Ground and continue to use Still Life's label, Sunflower Tribe.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Analogy was a German and Italian psychedelic rock, progressive rock band, active in the 1970s.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The band was launched by the guitarist Martin Thurn when attending the European School, Varese.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 1968, Thurn founded a band called Sons of Glove.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Other members were Wolfgang Schoene, Thomas Schmidt (later Pell Mell) and Jutta Nienhaus.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The band later renamed itself to Joice (due to a misprint later as The Yoice) in 1970 with drummer Hermann-Jürgen Nienhaus (brother of Jutta) and Mauro Rattaggi (bass), the only Italian member of the band.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" During a music festival in Arona, a spontaneous collaboration happened with keyboarder Nikola Pankoff whilst playing a free interpretation of Pink Floyd's "Atom Heart Mother".","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Pankoff became a band member thereafter.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Finally, in 1972, after becoming a more centered progressive rock band, they decided to change their name to Analogy.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Their first release was the single "Sold Out" / "God's Own Land", two songs written by Thurn.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" At the end of the year, Rattaggi had to join the army and left the band.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Schoene changed to the bass guitar.","title":"Analogy (band)"}],"peer_messages":["Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pre-Creedence is a compilation album by The Golliwogs which changed its name to Creedence Clearwater Revival (CCR) in 1968.","title":"Pre-Creedence"}
