{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Colt Express is a railway-themed family board game designed by Christophe Raimbault, Illustrated by Ian Parovel and Jordi Valbuena, published in 2014 by Ludonaute and distributed by Asmodee.","title":"Colt Express"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Cumberland Plateau is the southern part of the Appalachian Plateau in the Appalachian Mountains of the United States.","title":"Cumberland Plateau"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It includes much of eastern Kentucky and Tennessee, and portions of northern Alabama and northwest Georgia.","title":"Cumberland Plateau"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The terms "Allegheny Plateau" and the "Cumberland Plateau" both refer to the dissected plateau lands lying west of the main Appalachian Mountains.","title":"Cumberland Plateau"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The terms stem from historical usage rather than geological difference, so there is no strict dividing line between the two.","title":"Cumberland Plateau"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Two major rivers share the names of the plateaus, with the Allegheny River rising in the Allegheny Plateau and the Cumberland River rising in the Cumberland Plateau in Harlan County, Kentucky.","title":"Cumberland Plateau"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Colt M1877 was a double-action revolver manufactured by Colt's Patent Fire Arms from January 1877 to 1909 for a total of 166,849 revolvers.","title":"Colt M1877"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Model 1877 was offered in three calibers, which lent them three unofficial names: the "Lightning", the "Thunderer", and the "Rainmaker".","title":"Colt M1877"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The principal difference between the models was the cartridge in which they were chambered: the "Lightning" being chambered in .38 Long Colt; the "Thunderer" in .41 Colt.","title":"Colt M1877"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Both models had a six-round ammunition capacity.","title":"Colt M1877"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" An earlier model in .32 Colt known as the