{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":""You Open My Eyes" is a single by iconic Australian rock group Hoodoo Gurus which reached #43 on the ARIA Singles Charts.","title":"You Open My Eyes"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was released on BMG in February, 1994; and was written by Brad Shepherd.","title":"You Open My Eyes"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" This was the first Hoodoo Gurus single that was not written or co-written by Dave Faulkner.","title":"You Open My Eyes"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The B side, "Something I Forgot to Say", was written by Faulkner.","title":"You Open My Eyes"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The second B side was a cover of The Victims' "Television Addict" written by James Baker and Faulkner (both were members of The Victims and of Hoodoo Gurus).","title":"You Open My Eyes"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The music video for "You Open My Eyes" was directed by former Guru, Kimble Rendall and was their most expensive to date.","title":"You Open My Eyes"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Australian rock group Hoodoo Gurus have released nine studio albums, thirty-six singles, two extended plays, six compilation albums and four video albums. Formed in January 1981, the band was originally known as Le Hoodoo Gurus for the release of their first single, "Leilani", in October 1982.","title":"Hoodoo Gurus discography"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" As Hoodoo Gurus, the band signed with Big Time Records and premiered their debut album, "Stoneage Romeos", in March 1984.","title":"Hoodoo Gurus discography"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Also issued in the United States through A&M Records, the record remained atop the Alternative/College Albums Chart for four consecutive weeks, with it also becoming one of the most played albums of that year on the college network.","title":"Hoodoo Gurus discography"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The group's subsequent albums, "Mars Needs Guitars!","title":"Hoodoo Gurus discography"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":"", "Blow Your Cool!","title":"Hoodoo Gurus discography"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":"" and "Magnum Cum Louder", all reached the "Billboard" 200.","title":"Hoodoo Gurus discography"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"James Lawrence Baker (born 7 March 1954) is a rock musician from Perth, Western Australia.","title":"James Baker (musician)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He has drummed with several bands including the Scientists (1978–81, 1995), Le Hoodoo Gurus (1981–84), Beasts of Bourbon (1983–85, 1988–90), and the Dubrovniks (1986–94).","title":"James Baker (musician)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 2006 Baker was inducted into the West Australian Music Industry Hall of Fame.","title":"James Baker (musician)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Hoodoo Gurus were inducted into the 2007 ARIA Hall of Fame.","title":"James Baker (musician)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Dubrovniks were an Australian rock band which formed in August 1986 as The Adorable Ones.","title":"Dubrovniks"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Early in 1987 they changed their name, which acknowledges two of their founders, Roddy Radalj and Boris Sujdovic, birthplace in Dubrovnik, a town in Croatia.","title":"Dubrovniks"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Both Radalj and fellow founder James Baker had also founded Hoodoo Gurus (as Le Hoodoo Gurus) in 1981.","title":"Dubrovniks"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" All three had earlier associations in the Perth punk scene of the late 1970s.","title":"Dubrovniks"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The group issued four albums, "Dubrovnik Blues" (August 1989), "Audio Sonic Love Affair" (September 1990), "Chrome" (June 1992), and "Medicine Wheel" (1994), before disbanding in 1995.","title":"Dubrovniks"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Richard Grossman (born 28 November 1959) is an Australian rock musician who has played bass guitar for two iconic bands: Divinyls and Hoodoo Gurus.","title":"Richard Grossman (bassist)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Hoodoo Gurus' status on the Australian rock scene was acknowledged when they were inducted into the 2007 ARIA Hall of Fame.","title":"Richard Grossman (bassist)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" For Grossman, this was his second Hall of Fame induction in a row; the 2006 award was for his stint with Divinyls.","title":"Richard Grossman (bassist)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Often referred to as Rick Grossman, he has also performed with other Australian bands: Matt Finish, Ghostwriters, Persian Rugs, The Kelly Gang and Men At Work.","title":"Richard Grossman (bassist)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Leilani" was the first single by iconic Australian rock group Hoodoo Gurus when they were called Le Hoodoo Gurus and was released on Phantom Records in October 1982.","title":"Leilani (song)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It had been written by all four Gurus: James Baker, Dave Faulkner, Roddy Radalj and Kimble Rendall.","title":"Leilani (song)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Rendall left shortly before its release and, not long after, the band dropped the 'Le' to become Hoodoo Gurus.","title":"Leilani (song)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Le Hoodoo Gurus were noted for having three guitars and no bass player, creating a distinctive, layered sound.","title":"Leilani (song)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" This was captured on "Leilani", which told the story of a maiden sacrificed to the gods and an erupting volcano while her true love looked on helplessly.","title":"Leilani (song)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The song was later released on Hoodoo Gurus' first album "Stoneage Romeos" (1984).","title":"Leilani (song)"}],"peer_messages":["Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"\"You Open My Eyes\" is a single by iconic Australian rock group Hoodoo Gurus which reached #43 on the ARIA Singles Charts.","title":"You Open My Eyes"}
