s in New York, but subsequently changed their name to Graffiti and recorded briefly for ABC Records.","title":"The Hangmen (Washington, D.C. band)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Still Life was a 3-piece rock band originally from Moorpark, California and then the San Fernando Valley, California.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" They are credited for helping pioneer the first wave of what is often referred to as emo/emocore /screamo bands in this subgenre that is rooted in punk rock.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" They started in the late 80s with the name Monster Club.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" They changed their name in 1991 after the departure of the original lead singer Rick Rodney who joined the band Strife.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" They were active until 2003 when all of their instruments were stolen from Chris Pitzel's truck parked on the street in front of their house after a show.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Former Members Paul Rauch and David Pitzel have started a new band named Old Ground and continue to use Still Life's label, Sunflower Tribe.","title":"Still Life (US band)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Analogy was a German and Italian psychedelic rock, progressive rock band, active in the 1970s.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The band was launched by the guitarist Martin Thurn when attending the European School, Varese.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 1968, Thurn founded a band called Sons of Glove.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Other members were Wolfgang Schoene, Thomas Schmidt (later Pell Mell) and Jutta Nienhaus.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The band later renamed itself to Joice (due to a misprint later as The Yoice) in 1970 with drummer Hermann-Jürgen Nienhaus (brother of Jutta) and Mauro Rattaggi (bass), the only Italian member of the band.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" During a music festival in Arona, a spontaneous collaboration happened with keyboarder Nikola Pankoff whilst playing a free interpretation of Pink Floyd's "Atom Heart Mother".","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Pankoff became a band member thereafter.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Finally, in 1972, after becoming a more centered progressive rock band, they decided to change their name to Analogy.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Their first release was the single "Sold Out" / "God's Own Land", two songs written by Thurn.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" At the end of the year, Rattaggi had to join the army and left the band.","title":"Analogy (band)"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Schoene changed to the bass guitar.","title":"Analogy (band)"}],"peer_messages":[" Sunflower Tribe.","title":"Still Life (US band)"}
