{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Man in the Rain" is a pop song written and performed by English multi-instrumentalist Mike Oldfield.","title":"Man in the Rain"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was included on the album "Tubular Bells III" and released as a single on 5 October 1998 by Warner Music.","title":"Man in the Rain"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The vocals were performed by Irish folk singer Cara Dillon.","title":"Man in the Rain"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Moonlight Shadow" is a song written and performed by English multi-instrumentalist Mike Oldfield, released as a single in May 1983 by Virgin Records, and included in the album "Crises" of the same year.","title":"Moonlight Shadow"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The vocals were performed by Scottish vocalist Maggie Reilly, who had collaborated with Mike Oldfield since 1980.","title":"Moonlight Shadow"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It is Oldfield's most successful single, reaching number one on a number of charts around Europe.","title":"Moonlight Shadow"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Valedictorian is an academic title of success used in the United States, Canada, Central America, and the Philippines for the student who delivers the closing or farewell statement at a graduation ceremony (called a valediction).","title":"Valedictorian"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The chosen valedictorian is often the student with the highest ranking (highest Grade Point Average, or GPA for short) among their graduating class.","title":"Valedictorian"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The term is an Anglicised derivation of the Latin "vale dicere" ("to say farewell"), historically rooted in the valedictorian's traditional role as the final speaker at the graduation ceremony before the students receive their diplomas.","title":"Valedictorian"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" So the valedictory address generally is considered a final farewell to classmates, before they disperse to pursue their individual paths after graduating.","title":"Valedictorian"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The University of Utah (also referred to as the U, U of U, or Utah) is a public coeducational space-grant research university in Salt Lake City, Utah, United States.","title":"University of Utah"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" As the state's flagship university, the university offers more than 100 undergraduate majors and more than 92 graduate degree programs.","title":"University of Utah"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The university is classified in the highest ranking: "R-1: Doctoral Universities – Highest Research Activity" by the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education.","title":"University of Utah"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The Carnegie Classification also considers the university as "selective", which is its second most selective admissions category.","title":"University of Utah"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Graduate studies include the S.J. Quinney College of Law and the School of Medicine, Utah's only medical school.","title":"University of Utah"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" As of Fall 2015, there are 23,909 undergraduate students and 7,764 graduate students, for an enrollment total of 31,673.","title":"University of Utah"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"John F. Weston (November 13, 1845 – August 3, 1917) was a Union Army officer in the American Civil War and a recipient of the United States military's highest decoration, the Medal of Honor, for his actions during a raid on Confederate shipping.","title":"John F. Weston"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was a brigadier general of United States Volunteers in the Spanish–American War.","title":"John F. Weston"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Weston retired from the U.S. regular army in 1909 as a major general.","title":"John F. Weston"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Having been assistant commissary general of subsistence and commissary general of subsistence from 1897 to 1905, including commissary general for the American forces in Cuba during the Spanish–American War, Weston finished his career as the highest ranking U.S. Army officer in the Philippines and, in the last year of his service, as commander of the Department of California.","title":"John F. Weston"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"José Sisto, also called José Sisto Rodrigo and José Sixto, was twice Governor of Guam, first after overthrowing Francisco Martínez Portusach, and again after being legitimately placed in the position by the United States government.","title":"José Sisto"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He served as Spanish administrator of the Public Treasury in Guam until the United States captured the island during the Spanish–American War.","title":"José Sisto"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" When Martínez was named Commissioner, Sisto quickly staged a coup d'état and claimed the position as the highest ranking Spanish official on the island.","title":"José Sisto"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He began arming native guards and commandeering ammunition, but was briefly overthrown by Venancio Roberto and other pro-American elements on December 31, 1898, but was officially put into power by officers of the United States Navy only two days later after they decided he held a legitimate claim to the position.","title":"José Sisto"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" His second term was brief, and he officially relinquished control on February 1, 1899 after learning that the United States had obtained Guam in the Treaty of Paris.","title":"José Sisto"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" After giving up his post, he was found to have misappropriated public funds, arrested, and exiled to Manila.","title":"José Sisto"}],"peer_messages":["sentence_id":2,"text":" The university is classified in the highest ranking: \"R-1: Doctoral Universities – Highest Research Activity\" by the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education.","title":"University of Utah"}
